(a) $\mathrm{E}(X)=2 a$ (by symmetry)\\
(b) METHOD 1


\begin{align*}
& \text { uses } \operatorname{Var}(X)=\mathrm{E}\left(X^{2}\right)-[\mathrm{E}(X)]^{2}  \tag{M1}\\
& \operatorname{Var}(X)=\int_{a}^{3 a} \frac{x^{2}}{2 a} \mathrm{~d} x-(2 a)^{2} \\
& =\left[\frac{x^{3}}{6 a}\right]_{a}^{3 a}-(2 a)^{2} \\
& =\frac{13 a^{2}}{3}-(2 a)^{2} \\
& =\frac{a^{2}}{3}
\end{align*}


Note: Award as above if $\mathrm{E}\left(X^{2}\right)$ and $[\mathrm{E}(X)]^{2}$ are calculated separately leading to $\operatorname{Var}(X)=\frac{a^{2}}{3}$. Award (M1)AOAOAO for $\operatorname{Var}(X)=\frac{13 a^{2}}{3}$.

Question 6 continued

\section*{METHOD 2}
$$
\begin{aligned}
& \text { uses } \operatorname{Var}(X)=\mathrm{E}(X-\mathrm{E}(X))^{2} \\
& \operatorname{Var}(X)=\int_{a}^{3 a} \frac{(x-2 a)^{2}}{2 a} \mathrm{~d} x \\
& =\left[\frac{(x-2 a)^{3}}{6 a}\right]_{a}^{3 a} \\
& =\frac{a^{3}-\left(-a^{3}\right)}{6 a} \text { or equivalent } \\
& =\frac{a^{2}}{3}
\end{aligned}
$$

