A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis. The velocity of $P$ is $v \mathrm{~m} \mathrm{~s}^{-1}$ at time $t$ seconds, where $v(t)=4+4 t-3 t^2$ for $0 \leq t \leq 3$.
When $t=0, P$ is at the origin $\mathrm{O}$.
\begin{parts}
\part[7] \begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
\item Find the value of $t$ when $P$ reaches its maximum velocity.
\item Show that the distance of $P$ from $\mathrm{O}$ at this time is $\cfrac{88}{27}$ metres.
\end{enumerate}
\part[4] Sketch a graph of $v$ against $t$, clearly showing any points of intersection with the axes.
\part[5] Find the total distance travelled by $P$.
\end{parts}

\begin{parts}
\part[7] Prove by mathematical induction that $\cfrac{\mathrm{d}^n}{\mathrm{~d} x^n}\left(x^2 \mathrm{e}^x\right)=\left[x^2+2 n x+n(n-1)\right] \mathrm{e}^x$ for $n \in \mathbb{Z}^{+}$.
\part[3] Hence or otherwise, determine the Maclaurin series of $f(x)=x^2 \mathrm{e}^x$ in ascending powers of $x$, up to and including the term in $x^4$.
\part[4] Hence or otherwise, determine the value of $\lim _{x \rightarrow 0}\left[\cfrac{\left(x^2 \mathrm{e}^x-x^2\right)^3}{x^9}\right]$.
\end{parts}

explanation:
(a) (i) $\left(1+\mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} \frac{\pi}{6}}-1\right)^{3}$
\end{enumerate}

$$
\begin{aligned}
& =\left(\mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} \frac{\pi}{6}}\right)^{3} \\
& =\mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} \frac{\pi}{2}} \\
& =\cos \frac{\pi}{2}+\mathrm{i} \sin \frac{\pi}{2} \\
& =\mathrm{i}
\end{aligned}
$$

Note: Candidates who solve the equation correctly can be awarded the above two marks. The working for part (i) may be seen in part (ii).\\
(ii) $\quad(z-1)^{3}=\mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}\left(\frac{\pi}{2}+2 \pi k\right)}$\\
$z-1=\mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}\left(\frac{\pi}{6}+\frac{4 \pi k}{6}\right)}$\\
$(k=1) \Rightarrow \omega_{2}=1+\mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{j} \frac{5 \pi}{6}}$\\
$(k=2) \Rightarrow \omega_{3}=1+\mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} \frac{9 \pi}{6}}$

Question 12 continued.\\
(b) EITHER\\
attempt to express $\mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} \frac{\pi}{6}}, \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} \frac{5 \pi}{6}}$, $\mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} \frac{9 \pi}{6}}$ in Cartesian form and translate 1 unit in the positive direction of the real axis

OR\\
attempt to express $w_{1}, w_{2}$ and $w_{3}$ in Cartesian form

