$$M1A1

Question 12 continued.\\
(d) METHOD 1


\begin{align*}
& (z-1)^{3}=\mathrm{iz}^{3} \Rightarrow\left(\frac{z-1}{z}\right)^{3}=\mathrm{i}  \tag{M1}\\
& \left(\frac{z-1}{z}\right)^{3}=\mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} \frac{\pi}{2}} \\
& \frac{\alpha-1}{\alpha}=\mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} \frac{\pi}{6}}
\end{align*}


Note: This step to change from $z$ to $\alpha$ may occur at any point in MS.

$$
\begin{aligned}
& \alpha-1=\alpha \mathrm{e}^{\frac{\mathrm{i}}{6}} \\
& \alpha-\alpha \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} \frac{\pi}{6}}=1 \\
& \alpha\left(1-\mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} \frac{\pi}{6}}\right)=1 \\
& \alpha=\frac{1}{1-\mathrm{e}^{\frac{i}{6}}}
\end{aligned}
$$

Question 12 continued.

\section*{METHOD 2}

\begin{align*}
& (z-1)^{3}=i z^{3} \Rightarrow\left(\frac{z-1}{z}\right)^{3}=\mathrm{i}  \tag{M1}\\
& \left(1-\frac{1}{z}\right)^{3}=\mathrm{e}^{i \frac{\pi}{2}} \\
& 1-\frac{1}{z}=e^{i \frac{\pi}{6}}
\end{align*}


Note: This step to change from $z$ to $\alpha$ may occur at any point in MS.

$$
\begin{aligned}
& 1-e^{\frac{i}{6}}=\frac{1}{\alpha} \\
& \alpha=\frac{1}{1-e^{i \frac{\pi}{6}}}
\end{aligned}
$$

Question 12 continued.

\section*{METHOD 3}
$$
\begin{aligned}
& \text { LHS }=(z-1)^{3}=\left(\frac{1}{1-e^{i \frac{\pi}{6}}}-1\right)^{3} \\
& =\left(\frac{e^{i \frac{\pi}{6}}}{1-e^{i \frac{\pi}{6}}}\right)^{3} \\
& \left.=\frac{\mathrm{i}}{\left(1-e^{i \frac{\pi}{6}}\right)^{3}}=\frac{\mathrm{i}}{\frac{5}{2}-\frac{3 \sqrt{3}}{2}+\mathrm{i}\left(\frac{3 \sqrt{3}}{2}-\frac{5}{2}\right)}\right)
\end{aligned}
$$

Note: Award M1 for applying de Moivre's theorem (may be seen in modulus- argument form.)\\
$R H S=i z^{3}=i\left(\frac{1}{1-e^{\frac{i}{6}}}\right)^{3}$\\
$=\frac{i}{\left(1-e^{i \frac{\pi}{6}}\right)^{3}}$\\
$(z-1)^{3}=i z^{3}$

Question 12 continued.

\section*{METHOD 4}

\begin{align*}
& (z-1)^{3}=i z^{3} \\
& z^{3}-3 z^{2}+3 z-1=i z^{3} \\
& (1-i) z^{3}-3 z^{2}+3 z-1=0  \tag{M1}\\
& (1-i)\left(\frac{1}{1-e^{i \frac{\pi}{6}}}\right)^{3}-3\left(\frac{1}{1-e^{i \frac{\pi}{6}}}\right)^{2}+3\left(\frac{1}{1-e^{i \frac{\pi}{6}}}\right)-1 \\
& =(1-i)-3\left(1-e^{i \frac{\pi}{6}}\right)+3\left(1-e^{i \frac{\pi}{6}}\right)^{2}-\left(1-e^{i \frac{\pi}{6}}\right)^{3}  \tag{A1}\\
& =(1-i)-3\left(1-e^{i \frac{\pi}{6}}\right)+3\left(1-2 e^{i \frac{\pi}{6}}+e^{i \frac{\pi}{3}}\right)-\left(1-3 e^{i \frac{\pi}{6}}+3 e^{i \frac{\pi}{3}}-e^{i \frac{\pi}{2}}\right) \\
& =0
\end{align*}


Note: If the candidate does not interpret their conclusion, award (M1)(A1)AO as appropriate.

Question 12 continued.\\
(e) METHOD 1

$$
\begin{aligned}
& \frac{1}{1-\mathrm{e}^{\frac{\pi}{6}}}=\frac{1}{1-\left(\cos \frac{\pi}{6}+i \sin \frac{\pi}{6}\right)} \\
& =\frac{2}{2-\sqrt{3}-\mathrm{i}}
\end{aligned}
$$

attempt to use conjugate to rationalise

$$
=\frac{4-2 \sqrt{3}+2 \mathrm{i}}{(2-\sqrt{3})^{2}+1}
$$

$$
\begin{aligned}
& =\frac{4-2 \sqrt{3}+2 \mathrm{i}}{8-4 \sqrt{3}} \\
& =\frac{1}{2}+\frac{1}{4-2 \sqrt{3}} \mathrm{i} \\
& \Rightarrow \operatorname{Re}(\alpha)=\frac{1}{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

Note: Their final imaginary part does not have to be correct in order for the final three $\boldsymbol{A}$ marks to be awarded

Question 12 continued.

\section*{METHOD 2}
$$
\frac{1}{1-\mathrm{e}^{\frac{\pi}{6}}}=\frac{1}{1-\left(\cos \frac{\pi}{6}+\mathrm{isin} \frac{\pi}{6}\right)}
$$

attempt to use conjugate to rationalise

$$
\begin{aligned}
& =\frac{1}{\left(1-\cos \frac{\pi}{6}\right)-i \sin \frac{\pi}{6}} \times \frac{\left(1-\cos \frac{\pi}{6}\right)+i \sin \frac{\pi}{6}}{\left(1-\cos \frac{\pi}{6}\right)+i \sin \frac{\pi}{6}} \\
& =\frac{\left(1-\cos \frac{\pi}{6}\right)+i \sin \frac{\pi}{6}}{\left(1-\cos \frac{\pi}{6}\right)^{2}+\sin ^{2} \frac{\pi}{6}} \\
& =\frac{\left(1-\cos \frac{\pi}{6}\right)+i \sin \frac{\pi}{6}}{1-2 \cos \frac{\pi}{6}+\cos ^{2} \frac{\pi}{6}+\sin ^{2} \frac{\pi}{6}} \\
& =\frac{\left(1-\cos \frac{\pi}{6}\right)+i \sin \frac{\pi}{6}}{2-2 \cos \frac{\pi}{6}} \\
& =\frac{1}{2}+\frac{i \sin \frac{\pi}{6}}{2-2 \cos \frac{\pi}{6}} \\
& \Rightarrow \operatorname{Re}(\alpha)=\frac{1}{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

Note: Their final imaginary part does not have to be correct in order for the final three $\boldsymbol{A}$ marks to be awarded

Question 12 continued.

\section*{METHOD 3}
$$
\begin{aligned}
& \text { attempt to multiply through by }-\frac{e^{-\mathrm{i} \frac{\pi}{12}}}{e^{-\mathrm{i} \frac{\pi}{12}}} \\
& \frac{1}{1-\mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} \frac{\pi}{6}}}=-\frac{e^{-\mathrm{i} \frac{\pi}{12}}}{e^{\mathrm{i} \frac{\pi}{12}}-e^{-\mathrm{i} \frac{\pi}{12}}}
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\begin{aligned}
& =-\frac{\cos \left(-\frac{\pi}{12}\right)+\mathrm{i} \sin \left(-\frac{\pi}{12}\right)}{\cos \frac{\pi}{12}+\mathrm{i} \sin \frac{\pi}{12}-\left(\cos \left(-\frac{\pi}{12}\right)+\mathrm{i} \sin \left(-\frac{\pi}{12}\right)\right)} \\
& =-\frac{\cos \frac{\pi}{12}-\mathrm{i} \sin \frac{\pi}{12}}{2 \mathrm{i} \sin \frac{\pi}{12}} \\
& =\frac{1}{2}-\frac{1}{2 \mathrm{i}} \cot \frac{\pi}{12}\left(=\frac{1}{2}+\frac{1}{2} \mathrm{i} \cot \frac{\pi}{12}\right) \\
& \Rightarrow \operatorname{Re}(\alpha)=\frac{1}{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

Question 12 continued.

\section*{METHOD 4}
attempt to multiply through by $\frac{1-\mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i} \frac{\pi}{6}}}{1-\mathrm{e}^{-\mathrm{i} \frac{\pi}{6}}}$\\
$\frac{1}{1-e^{i \frac{\pi}{6}}}=\frac{1-e^{-i \frac{\pi}{6}}}{1-e^{-i \frac{\pi}{6}}-e^{i \frac{\pi}{6}}+1}$\\
attempting to re-write in r-cis form

$$
=\frac{1-\cos \frac{\pi}{6}-i \sin \frac{\pi}{6}}{2-2 \cos \frac{\pi}{6}}
$$

attempt to re-write in Cartesian form

$$
\begin{aligned}
& =\frac{1-\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}-\frac{1}{2}}{2-\sqrt{3}}\left(=\frac{\frac{2-\sqrt{3}}{2}}{2-\sqrt{3}}+\mathrm{i} \frac{\frac{1}{2}}{2-\sqrt{3}}\right) \\
& \Rightarrow \operatorname{Re}(\alpha)=\frac{1}{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

Note: Their final imaginary part does not have to be correct in order for the final $\boldsymbol{A}$ mark to be awarded

question_text:
A survey at a swimming pool is given to one adult in each family. The age of the adult, $a$ years old, and of their eldest child, $c$ years old, are recorded.
The ages of the eldest child are summarized in the following box and whisker diagram.
\begin{center}
\includegraphics[scale=0.1,height=4cm]{SV_IBDP_FST_AA HL_May 2022 _Paper 1_TZ1_1.png}
\end{center}
\begin{parts}
\part[3] Find the largest value of $c$ that would not be considered an outlier.
The regression line of $a$ on $c$ is $a=\cfrac{7}{4} c+20$. The regression line of $c$ on $a$ is $c=\cfrac{1}{2} a-9$.
\part[4] \begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
\item One of the adults surveyed is 42 years old. Estimate the age of their eldest child.
\item Find the mean age of all the adults surveyed.
\end{enumerate}
\end{parts}

Consider the functions $f(x)=\sqrt{3} \sin x+\cos x$ where $0 \leq x \leq \pi$ and $g(x)=2 x$ where $x \in \mathbb{R}$.
\begin{parts}
\part[2] Find $(f \circ g)(x)$.
\part[5] Solve the equation $(f \circ g)(x)=2 \cos 2 x$ where $0 \leq x \leq \pi$.
\end{parts}

Consider $f(x)=4 \sin x+2.5$ and $g(x)=4 \sin \left(x-\cfrac{3 \pi}{2}\right)+2.5+q$, where $x \in \mathbb{R}$ and $q>0$. The graph of $g$ is obtained by two transformations of the graph of $f$.
\begin{parts}
\part[2] Describe these two transformations.
The $y$-intercept of the graph of $g$ is at $(0, r)$.
\part[5] Given that $g(x) \geq 7$, find the smallest value of $r$.
\end{parts}

explanation:
\section*{6. EITHER}
attempt to obtain the general term of the expansion


\begin{equation*}
T_{r+1}={ }^{n} C_{r}\left(8 x^{3}\right)^{n-r}\left(-\frac{1}{2 x}\right)^{r} \text { OR } T_{r+1}={ }^{n} C_{n-r}\left(8 x^{3}\right)^{r}\left(-\frac{1}{2 x}\right)^{n-r} \tag{M1}
\end{equation*}


\section*{OR}
recognize power of $x$ starts at $3 n$ and goes down by 4 each time

\section*{THEN}
recognizing the constant term when the power of $x$ is zero (or equivalent)\\
$r=\frac{3 n}{4}$ or $n=\frac{4}{3} r$ or $3 n-4 r=0$ OR $3 r-(n-r)=0$ (or equivalent)\\
$r$ is a multiple of $3(r=3,6,9, \ldots)$ or one correct value of $n$ (seen anywhere)\\
$n=4 k, k \in \mathbb{Z}^{+}$\\
Note: Accept $n$ is a (positive) multiple of 4 or $n=4,8,12, \ldots$\\
Do not accept $n=4,8,12$\\
Note: Award full marks for a correct answer using trial and error approach showing $n=4,8,12, \ldots$ and for recognizing that this pattern continues.\\

question_text:
The continuous random variable $X$ has probability density function
$$
f(x)=\left\{\begin{array}{cc}
\cfrac{k}{\sqrt{4-3 x^2}}, & 0 \leq x \leq 1 \\
0, & \text { otherwise. }
\end{array}\right.
$$
\begin{parts}
\part[4] Find the value of $k$.
\part[4] Find $\mathrm{E}(X)$.
\end{parts}

Consider the complex numbers $z_1=1+b \mathrm{i}$ and $z_2=\left(1-b^2\right)-2 b \mathrm{i}$, where $b \in \mathbb{R}, b \neq 0$.
\begin{parts}
\part[3] Find an expression for $z_1 z_2$ in terms of $b$.
\part[3] Hence, given that $\arg \left(z_1 z_2\right)=\cfrac{\pi}{4}$, find the value of $b$.
\end{parts}
}

explanation:
(a) $z_{1} z_{2}=(1+b i)\left(\left(1-b^{2}\right)-(2 b) \mathrm{i}\right)$


\begin{align*}
& =\left(1-b^{2}-2 \mathrm{i}^{2} b^{2}\right)+\mathrm{i}\left(-2 b+b-b^{3}\right) \\
& =\left(1+b^{2}\right)+\mathrm{i}\left(-b-b^{3}\right) \tag{A1A1}
\end{align*}


Note: Award $\boldsymbol{A} \mathbf{1}$ for $1+b^{2}$ and $\boldsymbol{A} \mathbf{1}$ for $-b \mathrm{i}-b^{3} \mathrm{i}$.\\
\\
(b) $\quad \arg \left(z_{1} z_{2}\right)=\arctan \left(\frac{-b-b^{3}}{1+b^{2}}\right)=\frac{\pi}{4}$

\section*{EITHER}
$\arctan (-b)=\frac{\pi}{4}$ (since $1+b^{2} \neq 0$, for $b \in \mathbb{R}$ )

\section*{OR}
$-b-b^{3}=1+b^{2}$ (or equivalent)

\section*{THEN}
$b=-1$

question_text:
Consider the three planes
$$
\begin{aligned}
& \prod_1: 2 x-y+z=4 \\
& \prod_2: x-2 y+3 z=5 \\
& \prod_3:-9 x+3 y-2 z=32
\end{aligned}
$$
\begin{parts}
\part[4] Show that the three planes do not intersect.
\part[5] \begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
\item Verify that the point $\mathrm{P}(1,-2,0)$ lies on both $\Pi_1$ and $\Pi_2$.
\item Find a vector equation of $L$, the line of intersection of $\prod_1$ and $\Pi_2$.
\end{enumerate}
\part[6] Find the distance between $L$ and $\prod_3$.
\end{parts}

The $n^{\text {th }}$ term of an arithmetic sequence is given by $u_n=15-3 n$.
\begin{parts}
\part[1] State the value of the first term, $u_1$.
\part[2] Given that the $n^{\text {th }}$ term of this sequence is -33 , find the value of $n$.
\part[2] Find the common difference, $d$.
\end{parts}

Consider any three consecutive integers, $n-1, n$ and $n+1$.
\begin{parts}
\part[2] Prove that the sum of these three integers is always divisible by 3 .
\part[4] Prove that the sum of the squares of these three integers is never divisible by 3.
\end{parts}

A function $f$ is defined by $f(x)=\cfrac{2 x-1}{x+1}$, where $x \in \mathbb{R}, x \neq-1$.
\begin{parts}
\part[2] The graph of $y=f(x)$ has a vertical asymptote and a horizontal asymptote.
Write down the equation of
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
\item the vertical asymptote;
\item the horizontal asymptote.
\end{enumerate}
\part[3] On the set of axes below, sketch the graph of $y=f(x)$.
On your sketch, clearly indicate the asymptotes and the position of any points of intersection with the axes.
\begin{center}
\includegraphics[scale=0.1,height=10cm]{SV_IBDP_FST_AA HL_May 2022 _Paper 1_TZ2_1.png}
\end{center}
\part[1] Hence, solve the inequality $0<\cfrac{2 x-1}{x+1}<2$.
\part[2] Solve the inequality $0<\cfrac{2|x|-1}{|x|+1}<2$.
\end{parts}

Consider the binomial expansion $(x+1)^7=x^7+a x^6+b x^5+35 x^4+\ldots+1$ where $x \neq 0$ and $a, b \in \mathbb{Z}^{+}$.
\begin{parts}
\part[2] Show that $b=21$.
The third term in the expansion is the mean of the second term and the fourth term in the expansion.
\part[5] Find the possible values of $x$.
\end{parts}

A function $f$ is defined by $f(x)=x \sqrt{1-x^2}$ where $-1 \leq x \leq 1$.
The graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below.
\begin{center}
\includegraphics[scale=0.1,height=6cm]{SV_IBDP_FST_AA HL_May 2022 _Paper 1_TZ2_2.png}
\end{center}
\begin{parts}
\part[2] Show that $f$ is an odd function.
The range of $f$ is $a \leq y \leq b$, where $a, b \in \mathbb{R}$.
\part[6] Find the value of $a$ and the value of $b$.
\end{parts}

A continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $$
f(x)=\left\{\begin{array}{cl}
\cfrac{2}{(b-a)(c-a)}(x-a), & a \leq x \leq c \\
\cfrac{2}{(b-a)(b-c)}(b-x), & c<x \leq b . \\
0, & \text { otherwise }
\end{array}\right.
$$
The following diagram shows the graph of $y=f(x)$ for $a \leq x \leq b$.
\begin{center}
\includegraphics[scale=0.1,height=4cm]{SV_IBDP_FST_AA HL_May 2022 _Paper 1_TZ2_3.png}
\end{center}
Given that $c \geq \cfrac{a+b}{2}$, find an expression for the median of $X$ in terms of $a, b$ and $c$.

A biased four-sided die with faces labelled $1,2,3$ and 4 is rolled and the result recorded. Let $X$ be the result obtained when the die is rolled. The probability distribution for $X$ is given in the following table where $p$ and $q$ are constants.
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|c|}
\hline$x$ & 1 & 2 & 3 & 4 \\
\hline $\mathrm{P}(X=x)$ & $p$ & 0.3 & $q$ & 0.1 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
For this probability distribution, it is known that $\mathrm{E}(X)=2$.
\begin{parts}
\part[5] Show that $p=0.4$ and $q=0.2$.
\part[2] Find $\mathrm{P}(X>2)$.
Nicky plays a game with this four-sided die. In this game she is allowed a maximum of five rolls. Her score is calculated by adding the results of each roll. Nicky wins the game if her score is at least ten.
After three rolls of the die, Nicky has a score of four.
\part[5] Assuming that rolls of the die are independent, find the probability that Nicky wins the game.
David has two pairs of unbiased four-sided dice, a yellow pair and a red pair. Both yellow dice have faces labelled $1,2,3$ and 4 . Let $S$ represent the sum obtained by rolling the two yellow dice. The probability distribution for $S$ is shown below.
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|}
\hline$s$ & 2 & 3 & 4 & 5 & 6 & 7 & 8 \\
\hline $\mathrm{P}(S=s)$ & $\cfrac{1}{16}$ & $\cfrac{2}{16}$ & $\cfrac{3}{16}$ & $\cfrac{4}{16}$ & $\cfrac{3}{16}$ & $\cfrac{2}{16}$ & $\cfrac{1}{16}$ \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
The first red die has faces labelled $1,2,2$ and 3 . The second red die has faces labelled $1, a, a$ and $b$, where $a<b$ and $a, b \in \mathbb{Z}^{+}$. The probability distribution for the sum obtained by rolling the red pair is the same as the distribution for the sum obtained by rolling the yellow pair.
\begin{parts}
\part[2] Determine the value of $b$.
\part[2] Find the value of $a$, providing evidence for your answer.
\end{parts}
\end{parts}

In the following Argand diagram, the points $Z_1, O$ and $Z_2$ are the vertices of triangle $Z_1 O_2$ described anticlockwise.
\begin{center}
\includegraphics[scale=0.1,height=6cm]{SV_IBDP_FST_AA HL_May 2022 _Paper 1_TZ2_4.png}
\end{center}
The point $Z_1$ represents the complex number $z_1=r_1 \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} \alpha}$, where $r_1>0$. The point $Z_2$ represents the complex number $z_2=r_2 \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i} \theta}$, where $r_2>0$.
Angles $\alpha, \theta$ are measured anticlockwise from the positive direction of the real axis such that $0 \leq \alpha, \theta<2 \pi$ and $0<\alpha-\theta<\pi$.
\begin{parts}
\part[2] Show that $z_1 z_2^*=r_1 r_2 \mathrm{e}^{\mathrm{i}(\alpha-\theta)}$ where $z_2^*$ is the complex conjugate of $z_2$.
\part[2] Given that $\operatorname{Re}\left(z_1 z_2^*\right)=0$, show that $\mathrm{Z}_1 \mathrm{OZ}_2$ is a right-angled triangle.
In parts (c), (d) and (e), consider the case where $\mathrm{Z}_1 \mathrm{OZ}_2$ is an equilateral triangle.
\part[6] \begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
\item Express $z_1$ in terms of $z_2$.
\item Hence show that $z_1^2+z_2^2=z_1 z_2$.
\end{enumerate}
Let $z_1$ and $z_2$ be the distinct roots of the equation $z^2+a z+b=0$ where $z \in \mathbb{C}$ and $a, b \in \mathbb{R}$.
\part[5] Use the result from part (c)(ii) to show that $a^2-3 b=0$.
Consider the equation $z^2+a z+12=0$, where $z \in \mathbb{C}$ and $a \in \mathbb{R}$.
\part[3] Given that $0<\alpha-\theta<\pi$, deduce that only one equilateral triangle $\mathrm{Z}_1 \mathrm{OZ}_2$ can be formed from the point $\mathrm{O}$ and the roots of this equation.
\end{parts}

The following table shows the Mathematics test scores $(x)$ and the Science test scores $(y)$ for a group of eight students.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|l|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|}
\hline
\begin{tabular}{l}
Mathematics \\
scores $(x)$ \\
\end{tabular} & 64 & 68 & 72 & 75 & 80 & 82 & 85 & 86 \\
\hline
\begin{tabular}{l}
Science \\
scores $(y)$ \\
\end{tabular} & 67 & 72 & 77 & 76 & 84 & 83 & 89 & 91 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

The regression line of $y$ on $x$ for this data can be written in the form $y=a x+b$.\\
(a) Find the value of $a$ and the value of $b$.\\
(b) Write down the value of the Pearson's product-moment correlation coefficient, $r$.\\
(c) Use the equation of your regression line to predict the Science test score for a student who has a score of 78 on the Mathematics test. Express your answer to the nearest integer. \\

The function $f$ is defined as $f(x)=\ln \left(x \mathrm{e}^{x}+1\right)-x^{4}$, for $0 \leq x \leq 2$. The graph of $f$ is shown in the following diagram.

\begin{center}
\includegraphics[width=0.4\textwidth]{2024_09_19_293e963996f8a22efbbfg-04}
\end{center}

The graph of $f$ has a local maximum at point A. The graph intersects the $x$-axis at the origin and at point B.\\
(a) Find the coordinates of A.\\
(b) Find the $x$-coordinate of B .\\
(c) Find the total area enclosed by the graph of $f$, the $x$-axis and the line $x=2$. \\

explanation:
(a) $\int_{0}^{b} a x \mathrm{e}^{x} \mathrm{~d} x=1$ (seen anywhere)\\
attempt to use integration by parts (either way around)


\begin{align*}
& {\left[a x \mathrm{e}^{x}\right]_{0}^{b}-\int_{0}^{b} a \mathrm{e}^{x} \mathrm{~d} x(=1)}  \tag{A1}\\
& {\left[a x \mathrm{e}^{x}\right]_{0}^{b}-\left[a \mathrm{e}^{x}\right]_{0}^{b}(=1)}
\end{align*}



$$
\begin{aligned}
& a b \mathrm{e}^{b}-a \mathrm{e}^{b}+a=1 \\
& a=\frac{1}{b \mathrm{e}^{b}-\mathrm{e}^{b}+1}
\end{aligned}
$$

(b) $\int_{0}^{m} x \mathrm{e}^{x} \mathrm{~d} x=\frac{1}{2}$\\
$\left[x \mathrm{e}^{x}\right]_{0}^{m}-\left[\mathrm{e}^{x}\right]_{0}^{m}=\frac{1}{2}$\\
$m \mathrm{e}^{m}-\mathrm{e}^{m}+1=\frac{1}{2}$\\
$m=0.768039 \ldots$\\
$m=0.768$\\

question_text:
The following diagram shows liquid in a round-bottomed glass flask, which is made of a sphere and a cylindrical neck.

\begin{center}
\includegraphics[width=0.4\textwidth]{2024_09_19_293e963996f8a22efbbfg-10}
\end{center}

Initially, the flask is empty. Liquid is poured into the flask at a rate of $2 \mathrm{~cm}^{3} \mathrm{~s}^{-1}$. You may assume that the liquid does not reach the cylindrical neck.

The volume $V \mathrm{~cm}^{3}$ and the height $h \mathrm{~cm}$ of the liquid in the flask satisfy the equation $V=5 \pi h^{2}-\frac{1}{3} \pi h^{3}$.\\
Find the rate of change of the height of the liquid in the flask at the instant when the volume of the liquid is $200 \mathrm{~cm}^{3}$. \\

The cities Lucknow (L), Jaipur (J) and Delhi (D) are represented in the following diagram. Lucknow lies 500 km directly east of Jaipur, and JLD $=25^{\circ}$.\\
diagram not to scale
\begin{center}
\includegraphics[width=0.4\textwidth]{2024_09_22_90d6f6aea981080b3bdbg-04}
\end{center}

The bearing of D from J is $034^{\circ}$.\\
(a) Find J$\hat{D}$L.\\
(b) Find the distance between Lucknow and Delhi. \\

The total number of children, $y$, visiting a park depends on the highest temperature, $T$, in degrees Celsius $\left({ }^{\circ} \mathrm{C}\right)$. A park official predicts the total number of children visiting his park on any given day using the model $y=-0.6 T^{2}+23 T+110$, where $10 \leq T \leq 35$.\\
(a) Use this model to estimate the number of children in the park on a day when the highest temperature is $25^{\circ} \mathrm{C}$.

An ice cream vendor investigates the relationship between the total number of children visiting the park and the number of ice creams sold, $x$. The following table shows the data collected on five different days.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|l|c|c|c|c|c|}
\hline
\begin{tabular}{l}
Total number \\
of children $(\boldsymbol{y})$ \\
\end{tabular} & 81 & 175 & 202 & 346 & 360 \\
\hline
\begin{tabular}{l}
Ice creams \\
sold $(\boldsymbol{x})$ \\
\end{tabular} & 15 & 27 & 23 & 35 & 46 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

(b) Find an appropriate regression equation that will allow the vendor to predict the number of ice creams sold on a day when there are $y$ children in the park.\\
(c) Hence, use your regression equation to predict the number of ice creams that the vendor sells on a day when the highest temperature is $25^{\circ} \mathrm{C}$.

explanation:
METHOD 1
$Q_{1}=31.86$ OR $Q_{3}=32.14$\\
recognition that the area under the normal curve below $Q_{1}$ or above $Q_{3}$ is 0.25 OR the area between $Q_{1}$ and $Q_{3}$ is 0.5 (seen anywhere including on a diagram)

\section*{EITHER}
equating an appropriate correct normal CDF function to its correct probability ( 0.25 or 0.5 or 0.75 (A2)

\section*{OR}
$$
\begin{aligned}
& z=-0.674489 \ldots \text { OR } z=0.674489 \ldots \text { (seen anywhere) } \\
& -0.674489 \ldots=\frac{31.86-32}{\sigma} \text { OR } 0.674489 \ldots=\frac{32.14-32}{\sigma}
\end{aligned}
$$

\section*{THEN}
0.207564...\\
$\sigma=0.208(\mathrm{~mm})$

\section*{METHOD 2}
recognition that the area under the normal curve below $Q_{1}$ or above $Q_{3}$ is 0.25 OR the area between $Q_{1}$ and $Q_{3}$ is 0.5 (seen anywhere including on a diagram)\\
$\left(Q_{1}=\right) 32-0.674489 \ldots \sigma$ OR $\left(Q_{3}=\right) 32+0.674489 \ldots \sigma$\\
$\left(Q_{3}-Q_{1}=\right) 2 \times 0.674489 \ldots . . \sigma$\\
$2 \times 0.674489 \ldots \sigma=0.28$\\
0.207564...\\
$\sigma=0.208(\mathrm{~mm})$\\

question_text:
The following diagram shows two buildings situated on level ground.\\
From point P on the ground directly between the two buildings, the angle of elevation to the top of each building is $\theta$.\\
diagram not to scale

\begin{center}
\includegraphics[width=0.4\textwidth]{2024_09_19_fb39c450f09ec56db356g-04}
\end{center}

The distance from point P to point A at the top of the taller building is 150 metres.\\
The distance from point P to point B at the top of the shorter building is 90 metres.\\
The distance between A and B is 154 metres.\\
Find the height, $h$, of the taller building. \\

A particle moves in a straight line such that its velocity, $v \mathrm{~m} \mathrm{~s}^{-1}$, at time $t$ seconds is given by $v(t)=4 \mathrm{e}^{-\frac{t}{3}} \cos \left(\frac{t}{2}-\frac{\pi}{4}\right)$, for $0 \leq t \leq 4 \pi$. The graph of $v$ is shown in the following diagram.

\begin{center}
\includegraphics[width=0.4\textwidth]{2024_09_19_fb39c450f09ec56db356g-07}
\end{center}

Let $t_{1}$ be the first time when the particle's acceleration is zero.\\
(a) Find the value of $t_{1}$.

Let $t_{2}$ be the second time when the particle is instantaneously at rest.\\
(b) Find the value of $t_{2}$.\\
(c) Find the distance travelled by the particle between $t=t_{1}$ and $t=t_{2}$. \\

Consider the function $f(x)=\mathrm{e}^{x}-3 x-4$.\\
(a) On the following axes, sketch the graph of $f$ for $-4 \leq x \leq 3$.

\begin{center}
\includegraphics[width=0.4\textwidth]{2024_09_19_7972fe15c53a23b8c5f8g-05}
\end{center}

The function $g$ is defined by $g(x)=\mathrm{e}^{2 x}-6 x-7$.\\
(b) The graph of $g$ is obtained from the graph of $f$ by a horizontal stretch with scale factor $k$, followed by a vertical translation of $c$ units.

Find the value of $k$ and the value of $c$.\\

The following table shows the probability distribution of a discrete random variable $X$, where $a, k \in \mathbb{R}^{+}$.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|c|}
\hline
$x$ & 1 & 2 & 3 & 4 \\
