\item By sketching the graph of a suitable derivative of $f$, find where the cross-sectional radius of the bowl is decreasing most rapidly.
\item State the cross-sectional radius of the bowl at this point.
\end{enumerate}
\end{parts}
}

In Lucy's music academy, eight students took their piano diploma examination and achieved scores out of 150 . For her records, Lucy decided to record the average number of hours per week each student reported practising in the weeks prior to their examination. These results are summarized in the table below.
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|l|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|c|}
\hline $\begin{array}{l}\text { Average weekly practice } \\
\text { time }(h)\end{array}$ & 28 & 13 & 45 & 33 & 17 & 29 & 39 & 36 \\
\hline Diploma score $(D)$ & 115 & 82 & 120 & 116 & 79 & 101 & 110 & 121 \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
\begin{parts}
\part[2] Find Pearson's product-moment correlation coefficient, $r$, for these data.
\part[1] The relationship between the variables can be modelled by the regression equation $D=a h+b$. Write down the value of $a$ and the value of $b$.
\part[2] One of these eight students was disappointed with her result and wished she had practised more. Based on the given data, determine how her score could have been expected to alter had she practised an extra five hours per week.
\part[1] Lucy asserts that the number of hours a student practises has a direct effect on their final diploma result. Comment on the validity of Lucy's assertion.
Lucy suspected that each student had not been practising as much as they reported. In order to compensate for this, Lucy deducted a fixed number of hours per week from each of the students' recorded hours.
\part[1] State how, if at all, the value of $r$ would be affected.
